[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi everyone!</p>\n<p>I'm an undergraduate working on a thesis about <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D-module\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">D</span></span></span></span>-module</a> theory (algebraic geometry / representation theory side). I'd like to formalise core definitions and basic properties in Lean 4 as a thesis appendix, using Coutinho's <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/primer-of-algebraic-dmodules/87B8F8AB3B53DBA8A8BD33A058E54473\">A Primer of Algebraic D-Modules</a> as my primary reference.</p>\n<p>Given time constraints and the current Mathlib status, I am planning to focus on the <strong>affine case</strong> (the Weyl algebra <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n(k)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>) rather than the full sheaf-theoretic setting.</p>\n<p>I found <code>Mathlib.RingTheory.Derivation</code>, but this covers first-order derivations rather than the full ring of differential operators. I haven't found an existing formalisation of the Weyl algebra itself.</p>\n<p>Design options I'm weighing:</p>\n<p>1.  Define <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_1(k) = k\\langle x, \\partial \\rangle / (\\partial x - x\\partial - 1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mopen\">⟨</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.05556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mclose\">⟩</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.05556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.05556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> using <code>FreeAlgebra</code> and <code>RingQuot</code>.</p>\n<p>2.  Define <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_1(k)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> as the subalgebra of <code>Module.End k (Polynomial k)</code> generated by multiplication-by-<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">X</span></span></span></span> and formal differentiation. (Note: This representation is not faithful when <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">char</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{char}(k) &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">char</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>.)</p>\n<p>3.  Define <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_1(k)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> as a skew polynomial ring <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k[x][\\partial; \\sigma, \\delta]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.05556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mpunct\">;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">σ</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03785em;\">δ</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> (where <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03785em;\">δ</span></span></span></span> is the standard derivative).</p>\n<p>Questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is formalising the Weyl algebra currently achievable in Lean/Mathlib? Are there any known blockers (e.g., gaps in infrastructure for non-commutative quotients via <code>RingQuot</code>)?</li>\n<li>Is formalising just the affine case (Weyl algebra + modules over it) a meaningful contribution for the community, or would it be too narrow without the sheaf-theoretic generality?</li>\n<li>Which option above (or alternative abstraction) would be easiest to work with and most amenable to future generalisation (e.g., <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n(k)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>, or eventually <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">D</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{D}_X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.02778em;\">D</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;\">X</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> on schemes)?</li>\n<li>Are there any experimental branches, ongoing projects, or files I should look at before starting?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Any advice on scoping or entry points would be very helpful. Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 567747550,
        "sender_full_name": "Hongyu Wang",
        "timestamp": 1768306244
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would be very happy to see a formalization of the Weyl algebra in Mathlib4. You may want to look at the existing work on Clifford algebras for hints.</p>",
        "id": 567804897,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1768320653
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Perhaps something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→ₗ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TensorAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeylAlgebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TensorAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">TensorAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">algebraMap</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeylAlgebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">RingQuot</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">WeylAlgebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 567806630,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1768321107
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi everyone! I am here to post a small update of the old discussion here on the formalisation of Weyl algebra. First, thanks for <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"630477\">@Scott Carnahan</span> for pointing to the Clifford algebra for reference and thanks for <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"310045\">@Eric Wieser</span> for suggesting the construction.</p>\n<p>I now have some candidate implementations of this construction, universal property, basic APIs and some tests. It is extremely and closely modelled on Mathlib's existing <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/CliffordAlgebra/Basic.lean\"><code>CliffordAlgebra/Basic.lean</code></a>, adpted to commutation relation of Weyl algebra.</p>\n<p>The code is public, but I have not open a PR yet</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/WHY-Op109/mathlib4/compare/44f0a202c53528ecc62471822cc7f4a0d6a1e1a5...a8acf40843814fee8c697f693a0eb61660b0044e\">Full diff</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/WHY-Op109/mathlib4/blob/a8acf40843814fee8c697f693a0eb61660b0044e/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/WeylAlgebra/Basic.lean\">Basic.lean</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here are some <a href=\"https://github.com/WHY-Op109/mathlib4/blob/a8acf40843814fee8c697f693a0eb61660b0044e/MathlibTest/LinearAlgebra/WeylAlgebra.lean\">tests</a></p>\n<p>For LLMs/coding agents usage, I wrote the initial definitions and construction by hand in Febuary, and later used Claude Code to refactor the code toward Mathlib equality and convention by just prompting. In particular, Claude Code migrated the quotient construction from <code>RingQuot</code> to <code>(ringConGen (Rel B)).Quotient</code> and adapted the associated API and proofs, following Mathlib’s migration in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40451\">#40451</a>. I also use Claude Code and Codex to review the code and use Claude Code to write the test code primarily. Finally, I work and review through the code by myself.</p>\n<p>As this might be my first Mathlib PR, I am trying to keep the scope focused and make it as easy to review as possible.</p>\n<p>Would this specific definition and proposed initial scope be suitable for Mathlib? In particular, are there design or API changes worth addressing before opening the PR?</p>\n<p>Thank you for your time and advice!</p>",
        "id": 633004492,
        "sender_full_name": "Hongyu Wang",
        "timestamp": 1792416540
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is there any specific result you are aiming for?</p>",
        "id": 633381946,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1792514767
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don’t yet have a firm plan for a specific result now. My immediate goal is a Weyl algebra construction and basic API, following the Clifford algebra development in Mathlib where appropriate. Beyond this, I am also interested in the formalisation of polynomial representation, a PBW basis, and simplicity of the standard Weyl algebra over a characteristic-zero field. D-modules would be a possible longer-term direction.</p>",
        "id": 633544445,
        "sender_full_name": "Hongyu Wang",
        "timestamp": 1792566839
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You may want to consider defining algebraic differential operators following EGA4 section 16.8, so the Weyl algebra is the special case of a polynomial ring.</p>",
        "id": 633782046,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1792617646
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For D-modules, probably proving the local coordinate for differential operators is a reasonable goal. Another reference you might want to have a look is chapter 2 from <a href=\"https://math.bu.edu/people/yangzhe/BO_Crystalline.pdf\">https://math.bu.edu/people/yangzhe/BO_Crystalline.pdf</a></p>",
        "id": 633801972,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1792625101
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for the suggestions and references! This is a very good direction for future work: developing general algebraic differential operators, with the standard Weyl algebra as the polynomial ring case over a char 0 field. Would the current quotient construction, with its universal property and basic API, be useful as a standalone first contribution? Or should I first develop the general framework, assess how this construction fits, and then decide whether to contribute it separately?</p>",
